version: "2.0"
description: >-
  @a-company/gnomon-gui (spec §11; M1.10 slice 1) - the single-page app the
  kernel's loopback #web-endpoint serves as a hash-pinned bundle. Dependency
  light (no framework yet; React arrives with the Doodle canvas, §12.7);
  everything self-hosted, no runtime CDN loads (rule-6 CSP). scripts/build.mjs
  bundles src/main.ts with esbuild (gnomon-contracts inlined) into
  dist/bundle/{index.html, boot.js, styles.css, app.js}, hashes the tree
  (gnomon-bundle-tree:v1) and with --embed writes the generated
  packages/gnomon/src/kernel/web/gui-bundle.ts (opaque base64 + pin literal);
  --check (first step of `npm test`) fails when that file is stale.
  TD-2026-10-19-063: a session.open refused claude_binary_changed shows a
  [Re-pin] button in the red notice that focuses the kernel's pre-staged
  re-pin card (never decides); a claim-mismatch card renders its first line
  red and marks Deny as the default (focused) key.

components:
  gui:
    self-update: >-
      TD-2026-10-19-063 restart (core/restart.ts, PURE but injected storage
      and fetch): a pending daemon-stop{reason:'upgrade'} card shows the
      banner "Gnomon was updated · Restart now", whose button only focuses
      the card (the restart is the card's accept, one Touch ID). This page's
      own accept sets a single-use sessionStorage marker; the kernel's
      gui.resume{resumeToken, kernelPublicKey} (this connection only) is
      stored into that marker only. On close the page polls its origin
      (ORIGIN_POLL_MS cadence, ORIGIN_POLL_TRIES) and reloads; the reloaded
      page takes the marker once and attaches with {resumeToken}, re-pinning
      the successor key that came over the old verified channel. No answer:
      plain-language guidance (Gnomon.app, or `gnomon`).
    run-modes: >-
      P8-D86/D87 run-mode control (spec §11.4, §11.5): core/modes.ts (PURE)
      reads the focused session's mode ONLY from status.runModes (unknown
      values fail safe to manual), plans a choice (same | tighten | loosen |
      blocked with its ceiling reason), cycles ⇧Tab manual → ask →
      auto-edits → manual (skipping a blocked one) and builds the request
      log.append{kind:'request.mode.set', payload:{hsid, to} | {repo, to}}.
      ui/app.ts: a kernel-owned `mode: <mode> ▾` button + menu in the
      transcript header, ⇧Tab in the composer, palette mode.* (session) and
      mode.repo.* (repo), all on trusted gestures only (G-GUI-04); a
      loosening's pending strictness card is approved `allow` in the same
      gesture (the kernel asks for Touch ID), a refusal is shown as given;
      status is re-read when the store flags `modes` (approval.mode.set,
      request.mode.set, session.open, session.exited rows), never on a timer.
      mode.* commands are excluded from BLOCK_SAFE_COMMANDS. The transcript
      shows approval.auto as a subtle `auto-approved edit · <path>` line
      (`auto-approved · <tool> (always rule)` for a rule) and
      approval.mode.set as a system line. Tests: test/modes.test.ts.
    voice-controls: >-
      Voice slice V2d (TD-2026-10-19-298, T-2026-10-19-026; spec §11.4
      header, §14.7 attachment). core/voice.ts (PURE): voiceStatusOf is the
      one adapter over status.voice (V1 nested {attached: {voiceSessionId,
      hsid, mode, turns} | null}; V2a flat {attached: boolean, hsid,
      ceiling: ptt | conversation | null (the attachment's state, not a
      cap), since, idleAt, voiceSessionId, turns, echoTailMs,
      ambientIdleMs}, optional clientConnected, hotkey, level, maxMode);
      V2a refusals voice-off (cause aec | ceiling | presence) and
      voice-playback read as plain lines (voiceRefusalNote); an undecided
      widen card (strictness, op voice.widen) is focused in the stack; an
      attached session whose mode is not ptt reads as ambient
      (the indicator never under-reports a live mic). Three states: Mic off
      (grey), push-to-talk (slate blue, "Hold <hotkey> to talk"), ambient
      (red edge, pulsing dot, "Listening", level meter). clientConnected is
      only an explicit kernel field (null when unsure; no client-name
      guesses); attached with the app not connected keeps the attached
      state's class, label and meter plus "· voice app not connected"
      (never under-reports a live mic); "Voice app not running" only with
      nothing attached.
      planVoice: same | free (Off, narrowing, re-target) | presence (the
      first widening to ambient in a GUI login) | blocked (ceiling, nothing
      focused). voiceRequest builds log.append voice.session.start{hsid,
      mode: ptt | conversation, continues?} or voice.session.end
      {voiceSessionId?}. canAttach: only a human-class connection (a voice
      connection never gets the controls). ui/app.ts: the kernel-owned
      header pill + menu (Off / Push-to-talk / Ambient, Attach to focused),
      [Voice ▾] on the focused live session row, palette voice.off /
      voice.ptt / voice.ambient, all on trusted gestures (G-GUI-04); a
      pending card from an ambient request is approved allow in the same
      gesture only when isVoiceWidenCard (kind strictness, detail.op
      voice.widen, the same hsid) holds for the card in the store; else it is
      focused in the stack (the kernel asks for Touch ID). Status is re-read when the
      store flags `voice` (voice.session.start / .end / .mode, daemon.start),
      never on a timer; voice.turn.* rows only kick the meter. The kernel's
      idle auto-off (voice.session.end with reason idle) leaves a quiet
      "Ambient turned off after N min idle" note (store.voiceNote) until the
      next attachment. Transcript: a voice turn is the user message
      (one line, tagged with a mic and the speaker, capped at 32 chars) only
      when the kernel's turn.sent{source: voice, voiceTurnSeq} cites it;
      claude's replay of it shows once; until then the app's refusal /
      intercepted / forwarded claims are shown attributed ("voice app says:
      ..."); turn.sent adds its own line only when the voice row is
      unreadable, partials in grey while they are the latest word on their
      turn, a speaker marker under a spoken reply, a refused voice turn's
      reason inline and subtle. voice.* are excluded from
      BLOCK_SAFE_COMMANDS. Contrast: ambient text is the fixture's
      voice-armed pair (warn-ink on warn-bg), push-to-talk text the
      fixture's slate token; the pulse and meter stop under reduced motion.
      Tests: test/voice.test.ts; bundle pin in gnomon test/web-units.test.ts.
    voice-app-lifecycle: >-
      Voice slice V2e GUI (T-2026-10-19-026), SEAMLESS: no terminal step.
      core/voice-client.ts (PURE) is the one adapter over
      status.voice.clientConnected + status.voice.client {phase idle |
      installing | downloading | starting | running | crashed | unavailable,
      downloadMb {n, of}, devUnsigned, lastError, unavailable} and the
      enrolled list (status.voice.enrolled; absent = unknown, never a false
      nudge). Unknown phases fail toward not running.
      With nothing attached the pill shows "Voice app not running" [Start
      voice], "Setting up voice…", "Downloading voice models · N of M MB" with
      a progress bar, "Starting…", "Voice crashed" [Restart], "Voice
      unavailable · <why>", or (running, no enrolled voice) "Teach Gnomon your
      voice first" [Teach]; an attached live mic keeps its V2d state. The pill
      menu adds Start / Restart / Stop voice and "Teach Gnomon your voice…";
      palette voice.client.start / voice.client.stop / voice.enrol (never
      block-safe). Gestures are trusted-only log.append rows from a human
      connection: request.voice.client{op: start | stop};
      attach or Ambient while the app is idle or crashed sends start first in
      the same gesture. A LOUD role=alert banner "Voice is running an unsigned
      development build" shows when devUnsigned is true. The enrolment dialog:
      name (kernel rule [a-z0-9][a-z0-9._-]{0,63}, normalized), plain consent
      text, request.voice.enrol{name, consent} (answer {pending, cardId} =
      the Touch ID card, A4), Confirm with Touch ID (approve allow) FIRST,
      then the three phrases one at a time with a recording indicator folded
      from status.voice.enrolment (A3: requested, ready, recording, recorded,
      done, failed; reports stamped before the allow are ignored), re-read on
      every status read plus a light 1 s poll only while the dialog waits;
      closing while the card is pending denies it; enrolled voices with a
      two-click [Forget] sending request.voice.forget{name} (no card).
      Notices are status.voice.notice{text, code?, at}: a new `at` is a
      quiet toast prefixed "Voice app: ", never a decision or a backlog
      replay (30 s). Aegis L2-L4: the dev-unsigned banner is a persistent
      alert (no timer); "done" shows only once status.voice.enrolled lists
      the name; during the phrases the dialog says "Recording <name> — only
      <name> should speak"; isEnrolCard requires the card to name the voice.
      The kernel's client phases (stopped, preparing, installing,
      downloading-models, starting, running, backoff, failed, unavailable)
      map onto the pill's (downloadMb may be a total in MB). Kernel
      replies voice.turn.agent{source: kernel-speakable, items} show the
      speaker marker and swallow the voice app's next playback row (never
      shown twice). Tests: test/voice-client.test.ts.
    journal-cursor: >-
      Slice A4 (P8-D98): the hello offers journal.cursor; core/client.ts
      followLog pages then follows with the store's {logId, seq} cursor
      (GuiStore.cursor/advance/adopt); on BAD_REQUEST{cursor-epoch} (or a
      page from another log) GuiStore.reset() clears every row-derived
      state and the read starts over from the current log (once; a second
      refusal surfaces). Tests: test/gui.test.ts (journal.cursor).
    wire-rows: >-
      Dogfood fix 2026-10-19: core/store.ts rowOf flattens each log.read
      row as the kernel sends it (contracts LogRow: {seq, view:'full',
      event} | {seq, view:'stub', stub:{kind, hsid?, at}} | {seq,
      view:'oversize', kind, bytes}) before the reducers read kind, session
      and payload; reading the nested row flat left every row in the kernel
      feed (no session list, no transcript, and a TypeError painting the
      feed). turnEventSummary renders a turn.event line as the assistant
      text, its tool names and the result subtype. The composer says why
      nothing was sent (no session focused / not connected) instead of
      returning silently. Tests: test/gui.test.ts (wire rows, turn.event
      lines); the real-kernel falsifier is gnomon
      test/gui-session-e2e.test.ts; bundle pin in gnomon
      test/web-units.test.ts.
    session-resume: >-
      T-2026-10-19-014 B2 (§2.4 rule 11): the hello also offers
      session.resume (send/stop on a session the kernel resumed after an
      upgrade). GuiStore drops every card it holds on a new daemon.start row,
      so an old kernel's card id is never clickable (the new kernel refuses it
      NOT_FOUND and retires it with approval.expire). Tests: test/gui.test.ts
      (#gui store after an upgrade); bundle pin in gnomon test/web-units.test.ts.
    dogfood-layout-chat: >-
      Dogfood round 2 (2026-10-19). LAYOUT: the shell is a 100dvh flex
      column (header, notices, desk grows, status line); the desk is a
      three-column grid of panes that each scroll inside (sessions list,
      chat viewport, decision pile); under 900px one column, and sessions /
      decisions become drawers behind header toggles with a scrim (no
      horizontal overflow). The grid-row layout it replaces put the desk in
      an auto row once the hidden notices dropped out, so the app sat in
      the top ~290px. CHAT: core/store.ts chatItems turns a session's rows
      into the conversation: the user's message (this page's composer echo,
      else claude's --replay-user-messages line; session.inject / turn.sent
      carry only a hash), assistant text, one tool line per tool_use
      (toolSummary: name + command/path/pattern…), cards inline, the
      turn.result (subtype, cost, tokens) and lifecycle notes; hooks,
      stream_event partials, rate limits, status, control frames, tool
      results and bookkeeping rows are hidden and counted, and "show raw
      events" shows every row. A composer echo hides the matching replay so
      the list only grows at the end. STICKINESS: core/follow.ts
      (isAtBottom, Follow) — at the bottom it stays stuck through new rows
      and sends; scrolled up nothing moves it (not even a send), a
      "↓ latest · N new" key jumps back, and scrolling to the bottom by hand
      re-sticks; app.ts paints incrementally (no innerHTML, no
      replaceChildren) and anchors the row in view while heights are
      measured. STALE SESSIONS: GuiStore counts daemon.start boots; a new
      boot ends every live session the old kernel did not exit, the boot
      sweep's session.orphaned marks orphans, a session.open in the new
      boot (a resume) makes it live again, session.exited ends it (the old
      reducer matched a `session.exit` kind that does not exist);
      liveCount() feeds the Shuttle's live count. The kernel's
      status.sessions is still the constant {n: 0}, so the log is the
      source of truth. Tests: test/gui.test.ts (chat transcript, stale
      sessions, stickiness, layout CSS); gnomon test/gui-session-e2e.test.ts
      (real kernel: the chat is user/assistant in order, noise hidden) and
      test/gui-browser-darwin.test.ts (headless Chrome: desk ≥ 80% of a
      2000×1030 viewport, no horizontal overflow at 640px).
    description: >-
      #gui (spec §11.1-§11.5, §4.5). #bare-entry (§2.4 rule 8): the fragment
      may carry an optional `cwd` folder hint (base64url UTF-8, ≤ 1024 bytes;
      a malformed one drops the hint, never the launch); core/launch.ts
      cwdHintOf escapes and caps it for display and applyCwdHint opens the
      new-session form, shows it as text in #ns-hint, prefills #ns-cwd only
      when clean and absolute, and never submits — session.open stays on the
      human's click (test/gui.test.ts). Aegis round 1: the hint is applied
      only inside attach() after the launch token verified (never on a failed
      attach or Reconnect); clearCwdHint on cancel, trusted submit and "+ new";
      the prefill rule is the strict L/N/P/S-or-space rule minus Cf and
      default-ignorables, shared with the CLI via
      packages/gnomon/test/fixtures/cwd-hint.v1.json. static/boot.js reads the launch fragment
      (#launch=<token>&k=<spki>) and clears it with history.replaceState
      before any other script, handing it to app.js once. core/launch.ts
      decides the key pin (localStorage holds only the kernel public key;
      a changed fragment key re-pins visibly; Reconnect verifies against the
      pin; none ⇒ refuse); hostSurface() (T-2026-10-18-018 N1, M1.13) is
      'wrapper' only for the frozen non-writable non-configurable
      window.gnomonSurface the #gnomon-wrapper document-start script defines
      (a plain browser cannot produce it under the kernel CSP), and then the
      page sends hello surface wrapper and hides the extension-risk badge
      (display only). core/server-sig.ts verifies hello.serverSig with
      WebCrypto Ed25519 over authServerPreimage and binds serverKeyId; each
      hello carries a fresh clientNonce (getRandomValues, 32 bytes) that the
      result must echo (client-nonce-mismatch) and the signature must cover
      (T-2026-10-18-016); card
      decision keys only for kernel-signed actions that verify. core/client.ts
      is a MINIMAL in-bundle gnomon-rpc:v1 client on gnomon-contracts (hello,
      sig check, auth {launchToken}|{reconnect}, calls, notifications,
      gnomonPost for POST /diag and /blob with X-Gnomon-Token and
      referrerPolicy same-origin; per-call timeout; id:null error frames
      surface as a notice + /diag; a call written and unanswered when the
      socket closes rejects DELIVERY_UNKNOWN{delivery-unknown}, a call after
      the close CLOSED{connection-closed} (B7: one client-local spelling with
      the kernel + TS clients), T-2026-10-19-009 item 6; setup treats
      both as stopped) - swapped for the published #client-ts
      (M1.12) in slice 2. The GUI defines NO sanitizer (Aegis C1): every
      string goes through gnomon-contracts sanitizeDisplayText (display.ts,
      tested with conformance/display.v1.json through the render path, plus
      a fence test); core/render.ts renders the closed kit and kernel cards
      as DOM TEXT NODES only (no innerHTML); a block kit.Button is not a
      decision key and is live only for BLOCK_SAFE_COMMANDS (never diag.send
      or gui.reconnect). Card decision keys only for kernel-signed actions
      whose keyId is the hello's serverKeyId, cached per (cardId,
      contentHash) and cleared when the verify key changes. A re-pin is a
      sticky kernel-owned notice with old/new keyIds and a /diag line.
      core/store.ts reduces log.read rows into sessions by project, per-hsid
      transcripts and the decision stack (order independent, per-project
      filter); core/virtual.ts is the Fenwick-tree virtualized transcript
      with per-width height caches; core/commands.ts the palette, layout
      presets and theme/motion resolution. ui/app.ts wires the page: launch,
      Reconnect, backlog paging + follow subscription, sessions sidebar with
      new session (session.open), composer (session.send), decision stack
      (approve on trusted gestures; an ask-user card with a verified
      `answer` key gets a picker built ONLY from askDetailOf: the card block's
      one {type:'ask-user', detail} node whose detail recomputes (with kind,
      requester, hsid, flags; cardContentHash) to the card's contentHash —
      display text is never parsed; missing/malformed/mismatched => no
      picker (Aegis C1); radio/checkbox per option plus a free-text field
      (aria-label, maxlength = detail.maxAnswerChars), askNote (null-
      prototype answers) builds the
      approve note: plain text for one question, JSON {answers} for several;
      refusals shown verbatim until M1.3
      presence), Shuttle view, the kernel-owned extension-risk badge,
      papercraft day / phosphor night / auto themes and reduced motion.
    path: src/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, gui, paradigm-8, m1-10]
    tests:
      - test/gui.test.ts
      - test/modes.test.ts
      - test/voice.test.ts
      - test/voice-client.test.ts
      - ../gnomon/test/gui-endpoint.test.ts
      - ../gnomon/test/web-units.test.ts
  gui-setup:
    description: >-
      #gui-setup (spec §11.1, §11.2 rule 11; T-2026-10-19-005 MVP-b) — the
      first-run setup screens over the #setup-endpoint's closed
      gnomon-setup:v1 protocol: a `#setup=<tok>&k=<spki>[&cwd=…]` fragment
      (core/launch.ts parseSetupFragment; never mixed with #launch=) runs
      src/setup instead of the app. route.ts is the state machine (welcome →
      preflight → name → identity 1 of 2 / 2 of 2 → success → handoff; any
      refusal or a closed socket → aborted); hello is verified against the
      fragment setup key with the launch path's verifyServerSig before auth,
      and that key is never stored as the kernel pin. screens.ts renders by
      textContent only; clicks only request steps; the page sends only the
      name and a server-listed gen. digest.ts: the digest is the kernel's
      setup.presence value, shown big and grouped; the copy says the Touch ID
      dialog's last line must BEGIN with `Gnomon Helper: root key signs 1:
      <d12>`, that exactly 2 prompts come, and that cancelling is safe.
    path: src/setup/
    type: module
    gates: ["^setup-token"]
    tags: [gnomon, gui, setup]
    tests:
      - test/setup.test.ts
  gui-dictation:
    description: >-
      #gui-dictation (TD-2026-10-20-262, T-2026-10-19-026) — voice dictation
      into the composer. core/dictation.ts is a PURE reducer: a live
      voice.turn.user row with the kernel-stamped `forwardable: true`,
      non-empty text, no `intercepted`/refusal, for the attached session and
      past the start seq (no backlog; each seq once) appends to the draft with
      one space. One countdown ring runs over the per-machine delay
      (localStorage gnomon.dictation.delayMs: 3/5/8/15 s/off, default 5 s;
      pill menu picks and voice.delay.* palette commands) while the draft is
      unheld, the voice app idle (status.voice.dictation.activity), the
      session not streaming, and the attached session is the GUI's focused
      one (else the pill says "draft waiting"); new speech, transcription, a
      new turn or streaming resets it to the full delay. Hold: [Hold]/[Resume],
      the voice app's ⌃⌥H (status.voice.dictation.holdToggles, by parity),
      typing in or clicking into a draft. [Send]/Enter/⌘Enter send now. The
      send is session.send{hsid, content: one part per turn | the edit as ONE
      part, source: voice}. The #gui store reads turn.sent voiceTurnSeqs (and
      the older voiceTurnSeq), shows 🎙 for voice and 🎙✎ for voice-edited,
      and a forwardable unsent turn as "in composer". Only a human-class
      connection (canAttach) gets these controls; a voice connection never.
      Aegis conditions: hold records who set it (gui | voice | detached; a
      voice toggle never clears a GUI-set or detached hold); no live
      attachment for the draft's session ⇒ held 'detached', never
      auto-sent; an edit while sending keeps hold and the edit; turn text is
      sanitized by the contracts sanitizeDisplayText (sent as the voice app's
      exact words only when sanitizing changed nothing); the holdToggles
      baseline resets per voice connection (connEpoch, else
      client.restarts); edited drafts echo as typed messages.
    path: src/core/dictation.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, gui, voice, dictation]
    tests:
      - test/dictation.test.ts
  gui-table-talk:
    description: >-
      #gui-table-talk (TD-2026-10-20-294) — a reply's first speak fenced block
      (store.ts splitSpeak, mirroring the kernel projector) shows as a
      highlighted "said aloud" line at the top of the reply and is removed
      from the body; a reply without one (heuristic) gets no highlight. Lines
      that start with a bold label, in whatever scheme the agent chose
      (letters, numbers, short names), render as subtle chips (replyParts).
      A replayed user message drops the <gnomon-voice> guidance block.
    path: src/core/store.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, gui, voice, table-talk]
    tests:
      - test/table-talk.test.ts
